Always set to false, still add a space

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1042507/50747794-73c99400-1268-11e9-8ff5-f7c382559d20.png)


---

#### Issue #3008: Method Signature Markup for Declaring Reference Ownership Intent

C# is a managed language. One of the primary things that's “managed” is memory, a key resource that programs require. Programs are able to request memory from the system and that memory can be reclaimed automatically by the system's garbage collector (GC). The GC can only perform its job correctly when references, which is uses to determine if memory is used or available for collection, are managed correctly. Correct management of references means keeping control of the use and count of those references.

When references are passed to third-party code, developers are forces to take a leap of faith that method or class invoked does all of the right things: not keep referenced beyond the time they need them, not pass them to misbehaving code that keeps the references too long, etc. In many cases it is completely unexpected that a reference would be taken for any reason and it can cause unwanted memory leaks.

I propose a method mark up, similar to C++ pass by reference, which creates a contract that the method will not keep a reference longer than its scope and will not pass the reference to a method that might keep the references longer its scope.

```
public Matrix Multiply(Matrix& other)
{
    Matrix A = this,  B = other; // legal because B is scoped to the method body

    double t11 = A._1_1 * B._1_1 + A._1_2 * B._2_1 + A._1_3 * B._3_1;
    double t12 = A._1_1 * B._1_2 + A._1_2 * B._2_2 + A._1_3 * B._3_2;
    double t13 = A._1_1 * B._1_3 + A._1_2 * B._2_3 + A._1_3 * B._3_3;

    double t21 = A._2_1 * B._1_1 + A._2_2 * B._2_1 + A._2_3 * B._3_1;
    double t22 = A._2_1 * B._1_2 + A._2_2 * B._2_2 + A._2_3 * B._3_2;
    double t23 = A._2_1 * B._1_3 + A._2_2 * B._2_3 + A._2_3 * B._3_3;

    double t31 = A._3_1 * B._1_1 + A._3_2 * B._2_1 + A._3_3 * B._3_1;
    double t32 = A._3_1 * B._1_2 + A._3_2 * B._2_2 + A._3_3 * B._3_2;
    double t33 = A._3_1 * B._1_3 + A._3_2 * B._2_3 + A._3_3 * B._3_3;

    return new Matrix(t11, t12, t13
                      t21, t22, t23,
                      t31, t32, t33);
}

public Matrix Copy(Matrix& other)
{
    m_lastCopied = other; // illegal because m_lastCopied is not scoped to the method
    new Matrix(other.t11, other.t12, other.t13,
               other.t21, other.t22, other.t23,
               other.t31, other.t32, other.t33);
}
```

Since this would invalidate a lot of existing code, an alternative approach of marking references the callee intends to keep beyond its scope. Using a similar to, but different from, syntax from the C++ by reference syntax.

```
public void Add(T% item)
{
    m_internalList.Add(%item); // legal because the reference is marked as allowing retention
}

public void IsValid(string value)
{
    m_unnecissaryStringCache.Add(value); // illegal because the reference is scoped on to this method
    return value != null && m_validationRegex.IsMatch(value);
}
```

I offer the alternative because I'd like to manage the scope of passed references and I feel the common case is to not keep a reference beyond the scope of a method, and the case where a reference is retained is exceptional case.

